1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
getting into petty disputes with people

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:03,700
over very small amounts of money.

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:06,980
He's not even really retired,

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:08,760
so he has a decent opportunity cost,

5
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:11,560
but nevertheless, he will go out of his way

6
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,480
to go and say, I'm very angry about this here,

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:18,080
and I wanna talk to the manager.

8
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,080
Like, that's gonna do any good.

9
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,360
But that's the way that my dad is,

10
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,760
and he's not the only one for sure.

11
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:27,060
So plenty of people get angry at others,

12
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:28,800
and then wanna get back at them.

13
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,360
I'm gonna get back at them.

14
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,040
All right, so you may think about

15
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,440
whether you've ever wanted to get back at somebody.

16
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,280
All right.

17
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:38,360
So that's the idea.

18
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,080
All right, so real-world significance

19
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,520
of fairness and vindictiveness.

20
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:44,920
So pretty easy to observe.

21
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,200
So we wouldn't have tipping without fairness norms probably.

22
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,120
At least tipping would be very different.

23
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:50,520
There'd only be tipping in places

24
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:52,720
where there's repeated customers,

25
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:54,800
or of course, where you go in groups.

26
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,280
Now, the funny thing about the whole,

27
00:00:58,280 --> 00:00:59,840
the idea that people tip as much as they do

28
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,280
because other people are watching,

29
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,040
is actually restaurants seem to have the opposite theory.

30
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,840
Right, because it's very common for restaurants to say

31
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,920
that if the party has, say, six or more people,

32
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,560
what happens automatically?

33
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:10,760
The crudity's put in.

34
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:12,460
The crudity's put in.

35
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:13,920
So there it seems like contrary to theory,

36
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,280
that people only tip because they wanna go

37
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:17,500
and impress their friends with how generous they are.

38
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:18,840
Actually, when there's a large group,

39
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:20,600
the tragedy of the commons kicks in,

40
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,480
and everybody starts skimping,

41
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:25,000
because it will be the whole group

42
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,520
that will be regarded as an unfair group

43
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,080
rather than any one individual in the group

44
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,280
will be seen as unfair.

45
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:33,040
All right, so even when there's no repeated interaction,

46
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,520
we give up small personal benefits

47
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:35,760
to do what fairness requires,

48
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,720
expect others to do the same.

49
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,760
We might think that prices and wages would be more flexible

50
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:43,880
if we didn't have these fairness and vindictiveness norms,

51
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,160
because a lot of people have it in their heads

52
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:47,680
that it's unfair for prices to change,

53
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,880
and if a price unfairly changes,

54
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,120
it's okay to say shoplift,

55
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:57,120
or go and, say, buy something and then return it

56
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,440
and get your money back after you mess it up,

57
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:03,040
or something that was noted with a lot of foreclosures.

58
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,560
Very often, when a home is foreclosed upon,

59
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:08,360
what do the occupants do before they leave?

60
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:13,920
Trash the place, rip out the toilets.

61
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:15,600
It's one thing to go and try and recover

62
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:16,960
valuable items from the house,

63
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,560
like rip out the copper wire,

64
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,720
but just to go and put holes in the walls

65
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,080
to get back at the bank for taking your house away?

66
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,040
All right, this, in the end,

67
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,640
seemed like it was a pretty big deal,

68
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:31,360
and of course, it winds up having some bad effects,

69
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,760
because in general equilibrium,

70
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:35,160
the banks know that if they ever foreclose their home,

71
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:36,520
that you will trash it.

72
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:38,360
This does what to the interest rates

73
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:40,120
that everybody has to pay?

74
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:42,000
That increases, and especially, of course,

75
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,200
if you are a high-risk borrower,

76
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,680
high-risk borrowers have to pay especially high rates

77
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:47,360
because they're especially likely to foreclose,

78
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,120
which means that the bank's especially likely

79
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,220
to not get back its full investment.

80
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:54,460
All right, so things like this.

81
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:58,460
Now, one example that at least used to be talked about a lot

82
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:00,020
was shortages of concert tickets

83
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:03,220
when concert tickets would be priced well below markets,

84
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,580
especially for very exciting or big or important events.

85
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:08,180
I've heard people talk about this a lot less

86
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:09,980
than they used to.

87
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:11,340
Has it actually become less common

88
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:13,980
for tickets to be underpriced?

89
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:14,820
Or have you noticed?

90
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,900
So how common is it that, say,

91
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,140
tickets for popular concerts sell out

92
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,660
within a few minutes and then there's no way

93
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:22,860
of getting tickets?

94
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:23,820
It's still common.

95
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:26,700
It's still common, so maybe it's possibly less common

96
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:28,100
than it used to be, but again,

97
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:29,100
this is something where I can't tell

98
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:30,140
whether I've just gotten older

99
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,380
and so I don't talk to people who complain about it

100
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,380
or whether the world has actually changed.

101
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:36,120
It's certainly much easier to resell stuff

102
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:37,220
than it used to be.

103
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:38,060
Yeah.

104
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:40,060
So it may be that the actual harm of the norms

105
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:42,340
is much less because now they get bought up quickly

106
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:43,580
and then they get resold quickly,

107
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,260
and then in the end, it's still going to be rich people

108
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,940
who are the ones who attend the concerts.

109
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:50,620
So that might be what's going on.

110
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:51,940
Isn't there an embarrassment factor there

111
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:53,540
if you don't sell out?

112
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:54,380
I mean, you wanna-

113
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:55,940
Yeah, so that is a theory people are told,

114
00:03:55,940 --> 00:04:00,100
but normally people say there's sort of like a pride factor

115
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:02,620
in selling out, but couldn't there also be a pride factor

116
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:06,320
in charging a normal ticket price successfully?

117
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:09,120
So similarly, it seems like there could be a shame factor

118
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,340
in making your tickets really cheap, right?

119
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:13,740
So like if the tickets are a dollar, wow, we sold out,

120
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:15,300
but that's pretty embarrassing.

121
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:18,700
So I mean, there is a theory that you get some advertising

122
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:22,700
out of the news saying tickets sold out instantly, maybe,

123
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,500
but like, what's it feel like if they always sell out,

124
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:27,700
then why do you want that advertising?

125
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:30,140
It's not so clear what the point of that is.

126
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:30,980
All right.

127
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:35,500
So when we do labor economics, we'll talk more about

128
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,940
the role of fairness norms in labor markets.

129
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:40,180
You know, over time, I've become more and more convinced

130
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:43,740
that actually labor markets are heavily affected

131
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:46,820
by these norms and very much for the worse.

132
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,740
It would be far better if people would just have

133
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:52,020
a more Vulcan attitude about it and realize

134
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:53,940
it's only business, it's not personal,

135
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:56,260
there's no reason to get offended about the way

136
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:58,620
that people pay, and there's certainly no reason

137
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:00,780
to try to get revenge on someone.

138
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,700
And in fact, this would lead to higher levels of employment,

139
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,820
lower unemployment, more rapid recoveries and recessions,

140
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:08,260
but we'll talk about that more when we get

141
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:10,120
to the labor economics section.

142
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:13,380
All right, questions, questions.

143
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:15,620
Anyone bringing about anything?

144
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:18,740
All right, then let's move on to a topic

145
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:21,740
that I have researched and therefore super important.

146
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:22,940
This is an endogenous, right?

147
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:26,300
I wouldn't research it unless it's super important.

148
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:30,060
All right, now, have you ever heard an economist say,

149
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:32,420
preferences explain everything and therefore nothing,

150
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,220
or something like that?

151
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:36,620
All right, so, so you say, well, maybe what happened

152
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:39,820
is that people just started liking heavy metal music more.

153
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:40,660
Right, and then economists say,

154
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,220
no, that's not a good explanation.

155
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:45,580
You can explain anything with preferences.

156
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:46,500
And explain anything,

157
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:48,780
they didn't really explain anything at all.

158
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:51,380
All right, so this attitude all comes back

159
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:55,020
to an article by Gary Becker and George Stigler,

160
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:58,220
where they made an argument that is so odd,

161
00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:59,860
it really must be read to be believed,

162
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:01,460
but I will just tell you what they said,

163
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:04,260
and if you don't believe me, you can go and double check.

164
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:07,820
So what they said is, constant preferences,

165
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:09,980
or again, constant or also called staples,

166
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,340
stable, constant preferences, that's not really enough.

167
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:14,660
You know, and when it's not just preferences,

168
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:17,020
that's not enough, you know what we need to assume?

169
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:18,500
Identical preferences.

170
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:21,580
See, well, what do you mean

171
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:23,180
you're gonna assume identical preferences?

172
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:25,060
We're gonna assume that all human beings

173
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:27,700
throughout all human history in every country and society

174
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,260
have wanted exactly the same things

175
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:32,260
and have had exactly the same fundamental utility function.

176
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:36,420
But they totally don't, right?

177
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:40,060
Some people really like football and others don't, right?

178
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:41,900
And this is where Becker and Stigler say,

179
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:44,460
well, that's what a naive person would think,

180
00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:47,460
but the real story, the real story,

181
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:50,260
is that football fans face different constraints

182
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:53,980
than non-football fans, and that's what's going on.

183
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:54,820
Like what?

184
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,660
Well, I mean, they must have some kind

185
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:58,820
of different price factors or income,

186
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:01,100
a specialized income that they're having,

187
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:03,220
or it may be that they had more time

188
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:05,460
to build up a certain kind of human capital

189
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:08,780
that allows them to appreciate football games,

190
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:11,500
and then another person might have spent their life

191
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:13,740
building up the human capital to appreciate books,

192
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:15,860
but it's not that one person is a book person

193
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:17,420
and another one's a sports person.

194
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:19,700
It's that they had prior investments

195
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,220
which went and changed their production function

196
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:24,740
for utility, but fundamentally,

197
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:26,380
everyone's got the same function.

198
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:30,860
Now, I think they would be okay if you were to say,

199
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,340
like some people are born smarter than others,

200
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:36,260
and it's easy for a smart person to appreciate a book

201
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:38,420
than for a not-so-smart person.

202
00:07:38,420 --> 00:07:39,860
I think they might be okay with that,

203
00:07:39,860 --> 00:07:42,100
because they say, ah, so then it's the people,

204
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:44,180
everybody has the same utility function,

205
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,020
which is everyone receives utility

206
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:48,860
from understanding books that they read,

207
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:50,780
and then smart people can get the utility

208
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:52,700
because they're able to understand the books,

209
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:54,900
and not-so-smart people can't get utility from that

210
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:56,740
because they can't understand the books,

211
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:58,300
so then it's the same utility function

212
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,380
but a different constraint and the theory of sound,

213
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:03,300
and that's the way they put it,

214
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:05,300
and I think they actually, yes,

215
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:09,020
so I think this might be a footnote

216
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:10,500
where they talk about things like,

217
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,100
well, what about Jews keeping kosher?

218
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:14,900
Doesn't that just mean that Jews like keeping kosher?

219
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:18,140
And they say, well, that's not good enough, really,

220
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:21,620
because, well, first of all, they don't have to be Jews.

221
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:26,100
So you may say it's part of being part of that religion,

222
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:28,020
but you could just say, fine,

223
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:30,300
give me your theory about why they're in that religion.

224
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:32,020
Say, well, their tastes were formed at a young age.

225
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:33,460
No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

226
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:35,340
No, you can't say that your tastes are formed

227
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:37,080
by being raised in a religion.

228
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:41,100
That's violating the identical preferences assumption.

229
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:42,500
Now, if you were to go and reframe it

230
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:46,540
and say, fine, they built up Jewish human capital,

231
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:48,460
which other people don't build up,

232
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:51,420
and so the fundamental utility function is not different,

233
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:53,420
that's okay, although even there,

234
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:56,460
they'd be, wait, it sounds like you're making fun of me now.

235
00:08:56,460 --> 00:08:59,660
But any case, Stigler and Becker

236
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:02,060
were enormously influential with this work,

237
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:03,900
and even economists who have no idea

238
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:05,100
where this idea came from,

239
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:07,620
or often are themselves, whenever you say, well,

240
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:10,100
here's my theory about why people are doing it.

241
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:11,580
The people who are doing it like it,

242
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,340
and the people who aren't doing it don't like it.

243
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:15,980
Well, there's nothing else for economics to say.

244
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:18,540
Why is it that some people are eating the chocolate ice cream

245
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:20,140
and some are eating the vanilla?

246
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:24,420
And it's the same price for all the ice cream.

247
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:26,460
Some people like chocolate, some like vanilla.

248
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:27,700
It's in the utility function.

249
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:29,540
There's nothing more to say,

250
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:32,460
and yet I've heard economists say, oh, no, no, no, no.

251
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:34,280
You can explain anything that way.

252
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,220
Like then if you saw someone drinking motor oil,

253
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:38,220
you could say, well, he likes motor oil.

254
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:40,860
So, no, no, no.

255
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:43,380
There's gotta be a better explanation

256
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:45,620
for why some people are eating chocolate ice cream

257
00:09:45,620 --> 00:09:47,060
and others are eating vanilla.

258
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:49,820
And again, the story, and again, it comes down to,

259
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:51,580
it's not really, what you're doing isn't science

260
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:53,140
because it explains everything.

261
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:57,620
All right, now, what I see in response is

262
00:09:57,620 --> 00:09:59,580
there's actually a lot of empirical evidence

263
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:00,980
that's just not true.

264
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:02,940
So I have a paper in the Journal of Economic Behavior

265
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:05,380
and Organization on personality economics.

